# Placeholders for deferred features answer when you touch them

Sometimes a plan keeps a product affordance **visible** while its implementation is deferred, so the
idea is not silently lost. That placeholder has one job: tell the user the feature is not available
yet **at the moment they reach for it**, and change nothing.

## Required

- **Interaction is how the user learns.** The control looks and behaves like a control; pressing it
  shows a short message saying the feature is not available yet. People discover availability by
  trying things, and standing "coming soon" text next to every deferred control turns the UI into a
  changelog of what we have not built.
- **The message is a response, not a label.** It appears because of the interaction and goes away —
  a dialog, sheet, or notice. It is not permanent copy sitting under the control, and it is not a
  caption crowding an icon row.
- **i18n like any other copy**, in sentence case
  ([`ui-copy-sentence-case`](/.cursor/rules/ui-copy-sentence-case.mdc)). Prefer one shared "not
  available yet" string per surface over a bespoke sentence per control.
- **Nothing changes.** No state flips, nothing is written to storage, no request is sent, no spinner
  runs. A control that flips and forgets reads as a bug; a remembered setting the product cannot
  honor is a promise broken on every launch.
- **Accessible.** The control keeps a real accessible name, and because it is interactive it must not
  claim to be disabled. The message must be announced when it appears, not left for a sighted user to
  read.

## Mechanism per surface

Reach for what exists rather than adding a notification system for a message nobody wants twice:

- **web / management-web** — the existing modal or toast.
- **mobile** — a single-acknowledge dialog. If `ConfirmDialog` cannot yet express one action, extend
  it; do not introduce a second modal component or a toast framework for this.

## Don't

- Don't ship a **disabled** control with a caption explaining the absence. That was the earlier
  house style and it is no longer how we do this.
- Don't persist a value the product cannot honor, and don't add storage "ready for later" — the next
  agent will find the wiring and assume the feature half-exists.
- Don't hide the real state behind optimistic UI — nothing is pending, so nothing should spin.
- Don't let the message imply a date or a plan ("coming in the next release").
- Don't cite the deferral's plan, detail, or step number in a code comment
  ([`comments-future-forward`](/.cursor/rules/comments-future-forward.mdc)). The durable home for the
  decision is the detail doc under `docs/proposals/`; the file only needs to be honest about today.

## Where the reasoning lives

The placeholder answers "what does the user see". The **open questions** — tier, defaults, storage,
which surfaces get it — belong in the deferral's detail doc, which is what a later agent picks up.
If the deferral also constrains future work, give it a rule so the constraint is enforced rather than
remembered.

